UID:bigci-environmental-awards-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260802
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260803
SUMMARY:Deadline: BigCi Environmental Awards 2026
DESCRIPTION:BigCi's Environmental Awards are an annual prize for work that 
 engages with the environment\, open to Australian and international artist
 s working in visual art\, installation\, multimedia\, performance\, litera
 ture\, music\, photography\, filmmaking and curating. TWO WINNERS each rec
 eive four weeks of free residency at BigCi plus AUD 5\,000 in prize money.
  BigCi sits on the edge of Wollemi National Park in the Greater Blue Mount
 ains\, an hour and a half from Sydney but geologically another world\; win
 ners explore the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Wollemi\, Blue Mountains and
  Gardens of Stone National Parks on guided walks and are given research as
 sistance relevant to their projects. At the end of their residencies they 
 present the work at the BigCi Open Day. JUDGING: three independent judges\
 , Lea Kannar-Lichtenberger (Australia)\, Brian Wallace (China) and Dan Got
 tlieb (USA). APPLICATION: an online submission form (Wufoo) linked from th
 e awards page\, with a non-refundable AUD 40 entry fee paid at the time of
  submission. KEY DATES: submissions close 2 August 2026\; winners announce
 d 22 August 2026. Enquiries via the contact form at bigci.org/contact.\n\n
 Organisation: Bilpin International Ground for Creative Initiatives (BigCi)
 \nLocation: Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi 
 National Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\,
  about 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists
 .\nAward: Two awards\, each of AUD 5\,000 prize money plus four weeks of f
 ree residency at BigCi\, guided walks in the surrounding national parks an
 d research assistance for the project. AUD 40 entry fee\, non-refundable a
 nd payable with the submission.\nDuration: Four-week residency at BigCi pe
 r winner\n\nMore: https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://bigci.org/awards/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Bilpin\, New South Wales\, Australia\, on the edge of Wollemi Nati
 onal Park in the UNESCO World Heritage-listed Greater Blue Mountains\, abo
 ut 1.5 hours from Sydney. Open to Australian and international artists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:privacy-camp-2026-call-for-sessions@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: Privacy Camp 2026 (EDRi): Call for Sessions\, The Origins
  of Future Technologies
DESCRIPTION:Privacy Camp holds its 14th edition on 13 October 2026 in Bruss
 els and online\, organised by European Digital Rights (EDRi) with the Rese
 arch Group on Law\, Science\, Technology & Society (LSTS) at Vrije Univers
 iteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies at 
 Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center. 
 THEME: 'The Origins of Future Technologies: Towards communities of digital
  self-determination'. The call starts from the premise that digital future
 s do not appear from nowhere but are built through land\, labour\, capital
 \, infrastructure\, law\, public money\, political choices and social stru
 ggle\, and asks where technologies come from\, who gets to shape them\, an
 d what conditions make digital self-determination possible. It situates th
 is against the EU's 2026 Tech Sovereignty Package (semiconductors\, artifi
 cial intelligence\, cloud infrastructure and data centres) and the wider d
 eregulatory agenda including the Digital Omnibus\, the AI Omnibus and refo
 rms affecting data protection\, privacy\, labour and environmental safegua
 rds\, asking whose version of sovereignty is actually being discussed. TOP
 IC AREAS: (1) Material conditions in the production of technologies\, cove
 ring the natural resources needed across the supply chain (critical compon
 ents such as semiconductors\, water for data centres) and how GDPR\, ePriv
 acy and AI Act safeguards shape the material conditions of AI from data ex
 traction and biometric processing through to data centres\, labour and pub
 lic infrastructure\; labour conditions\, including who mines critical mate
 rials\, union-busting across the tech supply chain\, and coalitions betwee
 n tech workers\, unions\, migrant justice groups\, climate movements and d
 igital rights organisations\; and capital\, meaning the venture capital fo
 rces driving the current AI hype and consolidating through defence\, secur
 ity and militarisation. (2) Political vision in the use of technologies\, 
 covering military use and how militarisation and securitisation shape the 
 EU deregulation debate\, and the political ideologies driving technology d
 evelopment\, including what digital self-determination means for communiti
 es facing racialised discrimination\, surveillance\, occupation\, border v
 iolence\, workplace control or environmental harm. (3) Moving technologies
  forward through communities\, covering community innovation for digital s
 elf-determination (distributed and self-hosted data storage\, AI built by 
 and for community needs\, community-led\, public or cooperative digital in
 frastructure)\; law\, deregulation and rights (resisting the Digital Omnib
 us and specifically the Data Omnibus\, defending GDPR and ePrivacy safegua
 rds\, protecting access\, transparency and automated decision-making right
 s\, enforcing existing digital laws\, supporting collective litigation\, a
 nd challenging the framing of rights as obstacles to EU competitiveness)\;
  and building power by connecting grassroots movements across tech worker 
 organising\, labour\, migrant\, LGBTQIA+\, climate\, economic justice and 
 landback movements. FORMATS: interactive workshops are particularly encour
 aged\, meaning small-group work\, peer exchange and collaboration across s
 ectors\, coalitions and disciplines\, moving from talk to strategic action
 \; panel discussions are also welcome\, especially those centring diverse 
 voices and grounded lived experience with room for meaningful audience eng
 agement. SUBMISSION GUIDELINES: state a clear objective for the session an
 d what a good outcome would be\; make it as interactive as possible\; supp
 ort a diversity of voices and multiple perspectives\; average session leng
 th is 50 minutes\; propose the format that actually suits the idea rather 
 than forcing a panel into a workshop or vice versa. For workshops\, make t
 he objectives\, structure and facilitation clear and have participants wor
 k together in small groups towards a specific goal\; the organisers provid
 e additional guidance\, support and feedback for workshops. For panels\, l
 ist a maximum of 4 speakers covering academia\, civil society and decision
 -maker perspectives\, and indicate which speakers have confirmed at least 
 in principle. TIMELINE: proposals due via the online form by 10 August 202
 6 at 09:00 CEST\; the Content Committee notifies applicants before 21 Augu
 st 2026\; confirmation to proceed required by 25 August\; draft programme 
 published 4 September\; speakers and final session description confirmed b
 y 10 September at the latest (earlier confirmation improves the odds that 
 the EUR 400 reimbursement budget covers speaker travel and accommodation)\
 ; final programme published 28 September. The 2026 Content Committee is An
 dreea Belu (EDRi)\, Gloria González Fuster (LSTS\, VUB)\, Jill Toh (Racis
 m and Technology Center)\, Sanne Stevens (Justice\, Equity and Technology 
 Project) and Alexandra Giannopoulou (DFF). Programme enquiries: andreea.be
 lu@edri.org. Venue and reimbursement enquiries: guillermo.peris@edri.org.\
 n\nOrganisation: European Digital Rights (EDRi)\, with LSTS at Vrije Unive
 rsiteit Brussel\, Privacy Salon vzw\, the Institute for European Studies a
 t Université Saint-Louis Bruxelles\, and the Racism and Technology Center
 \nLocation: Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 
 2026.\nAward: NO speaker fee or honorarium. Where needed\, up to EUR 400 p
 er speaker or session organiser towards transport to and accommodation in 
 Brussels\, reimbursed against invoices and travel tickets\; those with org
 anisational support are encouraged to cover their own expenses. No budget 
 beyond this cap. Free to propose and free to attend (registration open).\n
 Duration: One-day conference on 13 October 2026. Average session length 50
  minutes.\n\nMore: https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://privacycamp.eu/privacycamp26-call-for-sessions/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Brussels\, Belgium\, and online (hybrid). Event on 13 October 2026
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alliance-gender-equality-strengthening-movements-2026@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260807
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260808
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alliance for Gender Equality in Europe: Strengthening Mov
 ements for Gender Equality (2026 call)
DESCRIPTION:NOTE: this is a gender-equality movement-building fund rather t
 han an arts or technology programme\, and it is coalition-only (no individ
 ual applicants). Relevance to creative or tech practice runs through the n
 arrative-change and digital-storytelling strand of the call. The Alliance 
 for Gender Equality in Europe\, a philanthropic initiative created in 2021
  and hosted by the Network of European Foundations\, is launching an open 
 call under its forthcoming Strengthening Movements Fund. The Alliance is c
 omposed of the Bodossaki Foundation\, Fondation BNP Paribas\, Fondation CH
 ANEL\, Fondazione con il Sud\, Fondation de France\, Fondation RAJA-Daniè
 le Marcovici\, King Baudouin Foundation and L'Oréal Fund for Women\; this
  particular fund is backed by all of those except Fondation BNP Paribas. O
 BJECTIVE: to foster collaboration and solidarity within European intersect
 ional and grassroots movements so that they can collectively advance gende
 r equality in Europe in the long term. The call follows a 2025 Alliance-co
 mmissioned mapping of European gender-equality movements (30 in-depth inte
 rviews plus contextual research) which found movements under-resourced bec
 ause philanthropic funding frameworks are misaligned with their needs\; th
 e fund is designed to replace short-term reactive funding with proactive l
 ong-term support. OUTCOMES SOUGHT: community organising reinforced at loca
 l or national level\; pan-European feminist collaborations and solidarity 
 strengthened\; alliances between gender equality and other social justice 
 movements expanded\; pro-gender-equality narratives and voices amplified i
 n local\, national\, regional or digital spaces\; policy debates informed 
 by feminist and gender equality movements. ELIGIBLE ACTIVITIES INCLUDE (no
 n-exhaustive): national\, regional or pan-European convenings and collabor
 ative projects where groups co-create joint strategies\; joint campaigns b
 etween feminist movements and allied movements (climate justice\, anti-rac
 ism\, LGBTQI+\, disability rights)\; narrative-change training for grassro
 ots groups covering framing\, messaging\, digital tools and digital storyt
 elling\; cross-border coalitions advocating EU institutions or national go
 vernments\; facilitated virtual or in-person convenings to work through in
 ternal tensions and build trust\; translating academic research on anti-ge
 nder efforts into movement strategies. ELIGIBILITY: collective proposals w
 ith at least 3 organisations per coalition (no maximum)\; legally register
 ed non-profits (NGOs\, civil society organisations\, social enterprises\, 
 national or regional funds)\; based and operating in the target countries\
 ; small and medium grassroots organisations\, defined by annual budget und
 er EUR 450\,000 (Austria\, Belgium\, Denmark\, Finland\, France\, Germany\
 , Ireland\, Luxembourg\, Netherlands\, Sweden\, UK) or under EUR 300\,000 
 (Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cypru
 s\, Czechia\, Estonia\, Greece\, Hungary\, Italy\, Kosovo\, Latvia\, Lithu
 ania\, Malta\, Montenegro\, North Macedonia\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\
 , Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Serbia). Intermediary organisations and ne
 tworks are exempt from the budget thresholds but must apply in coalition w
 ith grassroots organisations\, with the budget fairly distributed among al
 l partners. Coalitions must be community-led (majority of leadership roles
  held by members of the community supported\, and/or participatory approac
 hes)\, work with marginalised women\, transgender\, non-binary and gender 
 non-conforming people\, use gender transformative approaches\, and hold an
  intersectional perspective. Proposals must be completed in English. Each 
 organisation may submit only one application\, as lead or as partner. One 
 organisation per coalition acts as lead\, is the primary contact and is le
 gally responsible for the grant\; leads are invited to networking\, peer-l
 earning and capacity-strengthening activities. SCORING (100 points): relev
 ance to the call objective 20\; demonstrated track record in strengthening
  movements 20 (learnings valued as well as achievements)\; strong impact 2
 5 (must contribute to at least one of the five outcomes\, with clear measu
 rement)\; ability to work collaboratively and inclusively with partners 20
  (including fair budget repartition)\; quality and feasibility 15. PROCESS
 : two stages. Stage 1 opened 29 June 2026 and closes 7 August 2026 at 23:5
 9 CEST\, submitted in English via the online platform (a PDF of the form i
 s downloadable for reference only). Pre-selection results by email 14 Sept
 ember 2026\; stage 2 runs 16 September to 23 October 2026 and includes due
 -diligence contact from Alliance staff\; final results by email in Decembe
 r 2026\; grants start January 2027. The Alliance is piloting a participato
 ry selection process for this call\, with grant-making decisions made join
 tly by a committee of funders and non-funders holding equal decision-makin
 g power. Q&A webinars: 6 July 2026 at 14:00 CEST and 15 July 2026 at 10:00
  CEST (registration in advance\; both have passed). An updated FAQ is publ
 ished on the call page.\n\nOrganisation: Alliance for Gender Equality in E
 urope (hosted by the Network of European Foundations)\nLocation: European 
 Union\, United Kingdom\, Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina\, Kosovo\, Monte
 negro\, North Macedonia and Serbia. All coalition partners must be based a
 nd operating in these countries.\nAward: EUR 2\,700\,000 total across the 
 call. Between EUR 225\,000 and EUR 450\,000 per grant over 3 years (EUR 75
 \,000 to EUR 150\,000 per year). Project support including salary and over
 head costs\; overheads capped at 20 percent. 6 to 12 coalitions expected t
 o be funded. Free to apply.\nDuration: 3 years\, January 2027 to December 
 2029. Renewal possible at the end of the cycle subject to budget.\n\nMore:
  https://alliance-genderequality.org/call-for-proposals-strengthening-move
 ments-for-gender-equality/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://alliance-genderequality.org/call-for-proposals-strengthening-mo
 vements-for-gender-equality/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:European Union\, United Kingdom\, Armenia\, Bosnia and Herzegovina
 \, Kosovo\, Montenegro\, North Macedonia and Serbia. All coalition partner
 s must be based and operating in these countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-resynthesising-traditional-fragmented-distance-2026@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260808
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
SUMMARY:Deadline: Resynthesising the Traditional: Fragmented Distance (CTM 
 x NOPA Artistic Lab\, Armenia)
DESCRIPTION:Fragmented Distance is the latest artistic lab from CTM Festiva
 l's Resynthesising the Traditional initiative (co-curated by Stas Shariful
 la and CTM)\, taking place 1 to 10 October 2026 in Armenia in collaboratio
 n with NOPA\, a festival dedicated to sonic arts and experimental music. R
 esynthesising the Traditional explores how musicians\, sound artists\, and
  researchers can critically engage with traditions and heritage\, using re
 synthesis (a computer-music method that analyses sounds to extract their f
 undamental components) as a guiding metaphor\, and resisting conservative 
 views of culture as frozen or untouchable\; the lab is an open toolbox hel
 ping artists connect to 'the traditional' outside of exoticisation\, cultu
 ral essentialism\, and uneven power dynamics. This edition focuses on dias
 pora\, memory\, displacement\, transmission\, and hybridity\, responding t
 o Armenia's shift into a point of arrival for refugees and diasporic commu
 nities and to the displacement following the ethnic cleansing of Artsakh /
  Nagorno-Karabakh. It invites artists whose work resynthesising traditiona
 l practices addresses how traditional musical forms are maintained\, alter
 ed\, or recontextualised as they move across geographies and generations\,
  how in-betweenness and non-belonging can be articulated through sound\, a
 nd how sonic expression can act as rehumanisation. THE LAB (eight days\, 1
 0:00-19:00 daily) includes theory and practice workshops\, individual clos
 ed oral presentations\, solo public performances (15-20 minutes)\, special
 ist inputs\, guided sonic improvisations\, field trips\, and feedback sess
 ions. Following the lab\, participants may propose new solo or collaborati
 ve work to the Resynthesising the Traditional team\; selected works are sh
 ortlisted for commission and presentation at CTM Festival Berlin and/or NO
 PA Festival Yerevan. CONDITIONS: solo artists/projects only\; applicants s
 hould have an existing practice tied to (re)connecting with traditional id
 eas and sounds through a contemporary lens\, and clear next steps\; workin
 g knowledge of English recommended. SELECTION: a pre-selection committee (
 Varoujan Chetirian\, Stas Sharifulla\, Taica Replansky) creates a shortlis
 t\, from which up to six participants are chosen by lab co-hosts Cinna Pey
 ghamy\, Vardan Baghdasaryan\, and Varoujan Chetirian. TIMELINE: deadline 8
  August 2026 at 23:59 CEST\; results by early September 2026\; lab 1 to 10
  October 2026. Apply with a portfolio (PDF\, max 10 pages) and an audio li
 nk\; questions to team@nopa.art. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: CTM Festi
 val with NOPA Festival\nLocation: Yerevan / Armenia. Preference to artists
  who reside in or have a diasporic connection to Armenia\, Georgia\, Iran\
 , Iraq\, Syria\, the Anatolian region\, the Caspian Sea\, the Levant\, and
  the Northern Caucasus.\nAward: Participants supported with a travel budge
 t of up to EUR 450 each\, accommodation and catering for the duration of t
 he lab\, a NOPA Festival pass\, and a per diem. Visa fees supported when r
 equired\, and letters of invitation provided. Free to apply.\nDuration: Ei
 ght-day lab\, 1 to 10 October 2026 (arrival 1 October\, departure 10 Octob
 er)\n\nMore: https://www.ctm-festival.de/activities/resynthesising-the-tra
 ditional/artistic-labs/artistic-labs/open-call-nopa-festival?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/activities/resynthesising-the-traditional/a
 rtistic-labs/artistic-labs/open-call-nopa-festival?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Yerevan / Armenia. Preference to artists who reside in or have a d
 iasporic connection to Armenia\, Georgia\, Iran\, Iraq\, Syria\, the Anato
 lian region\, the Caspian Sea\, the Levant\, and the Northern Caucasus.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ctm-research-networking-day-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: CTM 2027 Research Networking Day: Open Call for Presentat
 ions (Berlin)
DESCRIPTION:The Research Networking Day (RND) is an exchange platform for g
 raduate or postgraduate students and independent artists conducting self-g
 uided research traversing audio\, arts\, media\, design\, and related theo
 retical disciplines. The RND 2027 open call seeks innovative and critical 
 submissions from all areas of study addressing the CTM 2027 festival theme
  'Process...ing'. Nine selected candidates give short ten-minute presentat
 ions within different modules\, linked by discussion rounds at the end of 
 each module\, offering an international platform to exchange with colleagu
 es and researchers working on related ideas\; research is also shared via 
 audio/video after the festival on CTM's YouTube and SoundCloud channels. T
 his edition takes place in collaboration with the Design Academy Eindhoven
 \, Universitat der Kunste Berlin (UdK)\, and Leuphana University Luneburg.
  CONDITIONS: presentations take place on-site within the festival's Discou
 rse programme\, in English\; applications are for individual (solo) presen
 tations only (no duos or groups). As in previous years\, no funding is ava
 ilable for travel and accommodation\; participants receive a CTM 2027 fest
 ival pass\, a catered lunch on the day\, and a group dinner. Entrance to t
 he event is free. KEY DATES: application deadline 25 October 2026 at 23:59
  CET\; programme announced mid to late November 2026\; RND 2027 event Sund
 ay 24 January 2027\; CTM 2027 runs 22 to 31 January 2027.\n\nOrganisation:
  CTM Festival\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany. On-site presentations within th
 e CTM festival's Discourse programme\, in English.\nAward: No funding for 
 travel or accommodation. Participants receive a CTM 2027 festival pass\, a
  catered lunch on the day of presentations\, and a group dinner. Entrance 
 to the event is free\; free to apply.\nDuration: One-day event\, Sunday 24
  January 2027 (within CTM 2027\, 22-31 January 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.
 ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/research-networking-day-2027?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ctm-festival.de/festival-2027/open-calls/research-networkin
 g-day-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany. On-site presentations within the CTM festival's 
 Discourse programme\, in English.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:design-trust-seed-feature-impact-grants-2026@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261020
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261021
SUMMARY:Deadline: Design Trust Seed\, Feature & Impact Grants (Hong Kong / 
 Greater Bay Area)
DESCRIPTION:Design Trust offers grants to individual designers\, curators\,
  collectives\, and non-profit organisations for projects and activities re
 levant to various design disciplines\, supporting work relevant to the con
 text and content of Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area (the Special Admini
 strative Regions of Hong Kong and Macao and the nine Guangdong municipalit
 ies of Guangzhou\, Shenzhen\, Zhuhai\, Foshan\, Huizhou\, Dongguan\, Zhong
 shan\, Jiangmen\, and Zhaoqing). It supports innovative\, thought-provokin
 g investigations across design disciplines from graphics\, media\, and wea
 rables to architecture and the built environment\, including exhibitions\,
  research projects\, and creative installations that advocate the positive
  role of design in the region. THREE GRANTS: (1) SEED GRANT (HKD 50\,000) 
 fosters experimentation\, testing\, and sharing\, awarded to individuals k
 ick-starting a meaningful project with social\, educational\, economical\,
  or environmental impact\; supports emerging designers\, young scholars\, 
 and projects in the pipeline needing additional resources\, including rese
 arch and prototypes focused on Hong Kong and Greater Bay Area issues to be
  showcased at international venues\, symposia\, or biennales. (2) FEATURE 
 GRANT (capped at HKD 300\,000\, awarded annually upon special consideratio
 n) fosters cross-disciplinary projects on the Greater Bay Area with wide-r
 eaching audiences\, often sited at an exhibition or biennale of internatio
 nal standing\, supporting exchange between a Hong Kong/China-based collect
 ive and an internationally renowned cultural institution. (3) IMPACT GRANT
  supports public-facing\, community-based projects led by former Design Tr
 ust grant recipients\, fellows\, and mentees with a proven track record\; 
 considered case-by-case (contact grantapplication@designtrust.hk). ELIGIBI
 LITY: individuals\, collectives\, and certified non-profit organisations e
 ither based in the Greater Bay Area or seeking support for design projects
  related to the region. Individuals or collectives who have completed two 
 Feature Grants or three Seed Grants should apply for the Impact Grant inst
 ead. APPLICATION: two cycles annually\, deadlines 20 April and 20 October 
 (late submissions roll to the next cycle)\; apply at least 3 to 6 months b
 efore the project's commencement. Design Trust may provide full or partial
  funding on a case-by-case basis.\n\nOrganisation: Design Trust\nLocation:
  Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area. Open to applicants based in the regio
 n or seeking support for design projects related to it\, including interna
 tional applicants.\nAward: Seed Grant: HKD 50\,000. Feature Grant: capped 
 at HKD 300\,000\, awarded annually upon special consideration. Impact Gran
 t: case-by-case\, for former grantees. Full or partial funding on a case-b
 y-case basis. Free to apply.\nDuration: Two grant cycles annually (deadlin
 es 20 April and 20 October)\; apply 3 to 6 months before project commencem
 ent\n\nMore: https://designtrust.hk/grants/dtgrants/?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://designtrust.hk/grants/dtgrants/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hong Kong and the Greater Bay Area. Open to applicants based in th
 e region or seeking support for design projects related to it\, including 
 international applicants.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:developh-tropical-internet-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260822
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260823
SUMMARY:Deadline: Developh Tropical Internet Research Fellowship (Philippin
 es / Southeast Asia)
DESCRIPTION:Developh invites applications for its inaugural Tropical Intern
 et Research Fellowship\, supporting researchers\, writers\, and artist-tec
 hnologists at any career stage who are investigating the cultural and mate
 rial conditions of the internet across the Philippines and Southeast Asia 
 and are interested in producing written work. The fellowship runs seasonal
 ly (every 1-2 years) to support new writing about the 'tropical internet':
  the online as experienced from the tropical regions of the Philippines an
 d broader Southeast Asia\, concerned with heat\, extremes\, monsoons\, dis
 tance\, islandedness\, improvisation\, pluralities\, and diasporas. It nur
 tures work examining how the internet is lived\, built\, resisted\, and fe
 lt across the region\, welcoming research on infrastructures\, digital lab
 or\, rural-urban internet inequities\, algorithmic cultures\, online intim
 acies\, and the entangled relationships between land\, technology\, and th
 e body\, and how colonial\, corporate\, and extractive forces shape infras
 tructures for connection. WHO SHOULD APPLY: researchers\, writers\, design
 ers\, artists\, and technologists with strong ties to the Philippines or b
 roader Southeast Asia\; applicants welcome from any present location\, wit
 h preference to those within the Philippines\, particularly outside Metro 
 Manila. Work may relate to an academic thesis\, but the produced artifact 
 must be a distinct new work separate from the thesis. COMMITMENTS: produce
  an output by January 2027 (suggested minimum ~2000 words\, in English\, F
 ilipino\, or any Philippine language)\; publish with Developh by March-Apr
 il 2027 with editorial and publication support\; present findings by March
 -April 2027\; share citations and research notes for a collective referenc
 e library\; attend three cohort group meetings\; and participate in editor
 ial review and feedback cycles (prototypes and interim essays are encourag
 ed\; a fully finished output is not required). FELLOWS RECEIVE: a PHP 20\,
 000 (~USD 300) stipend\, with a potential additional stipend depending on 
 fieldwork/research needs\; peer learning through online cohort sessions\; 
 check-ins with the Developh team and guest critics\; editorial and publish
 ing support (coding\, design\, hosting)\; and free access to Developh even
 ts and workshops for the fellowship period. TIMELINE: application deadline
  22 August 2026\; writing and research September 2026 to January 2027\; pu
 blication and presentations March 2027 onwards. Apply via the Google Form.
  Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Developh\nLocation: Remote / Philippines 
 and broader Southeast Asia. Applicants welcome from any location\; prefere
 nce to those within the Philippines\, particularly outside Metro Manila.\n
 Award: PHP 20\,000 (~USD 300) stipend\, with a potential additional stipen
 d depending on fieldwork/research needs. Plus editorial and publishing sup
 port (coding\, design\, hosting for the final output)\, online cohort sess
 ions\, check-ins with the Developh team and guest critics\, and free acces
 s to Developh events and workshops for the fellowship period. Free to appl
 y.\nDuration: Research and writing September 2026 to January 2027 (5 month
 s)\; publication and presentations March to April 2027 onwards\n\nMore: ht
 tps://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSffbGYSmGNCPuFxN_7qUiAUMd9PlA3QtiIg
 5OBUHxBsRyoIpw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSffbGYSmGNCPuFxN_7qUiAUMd9PlA3
 QtiIg5OBUHxBsRyoIpw/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote / Philippines and broader Southeast Asia. Applicants welcom
 e from any location\; preference to those within the Philippines\, particu
 larly outside Metro Manila.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:latitude-air-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: LATITUDE Artist in Residence 2027 (Chicago)
DESCRIPTION:The LATITUDE Artist in Residence program (started in 2013) is a
  one-month production residency where 8-10 artists are granted full access
  to LATITUDE's facilities and community to develop their practice. Its foc
 us is supporting artists who want to use LATITUDE's printing and scanning 
 services to expand their practice\, regardless of medium or experience\; p
 ast residents include photographers\, painters\, performance artists\, new
  media artists\, and sculptors. WHAT RESIDENTS RECEIVE: a $1\,000 printing
  stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal studio with a calibrated workst
 ation and 24/7 access\, training and guidance on tech from staff\, an orga
 nised public event to present new work\, and lifetime free lab access. LAT
 ITUDE does not offer a housing stipend but will help schedule studio visit
 s during the month. PUBLIC PROGRAMMING: during the residency each artist p
 uts on one community programming event (past examples include panel discus
 sions\, artist talks\, performances\, workshops\, and critiques\; a galler
 y space is available for curation) and is asked to act as a mentor to the 
 community\, sharing insights and creative methods. WHO SHOULD APPLY: artis
 ts interested in production\, education\, and tech experimentation\; no pr
 ior printing experience\, art education\, or media specificity is required
 \, though professionalism and communication are expected. 2027 PANEL COMMI
 TTEE: Yechen Zhao\, B. Ingrid Olson\, Teri Carson. TIMELINE: applications 
 open 1 August 2026 (free before 16 August\, a $20 donation thereafter) and
  close 1 September 2026 at 12pm (noon) CST\; the selection committee revie
 ws in September\, meets to decide finalists in October\, and acceptances a
 re sent early November 2026. Apply at latitudechicago.org/artist-in-reside
 nce.\n\nOrganisation: LATITUDE Chicago\nLocation: Chicago\, Illinois\, USA
 . No housing stipend offered.\nAward: One-month production residency for 8
 -10 artists: a $1\,000 printing stipend\, unlimited scanning\, a personal 
 studio with a calibrated workstation\, staff training and guidance on tech
 \, an organised public event\, and lifetime free lab access. No housing st
 ipend. Free to apply before 16 August 2026\; a $20 donation thereafter.\nD
 uration: One month (residency takes place in 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.la
 titudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.latitudechicago.org/artist-in-residence?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Chicago\, Illinois\, USA. No housing stipend offered.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gmap-digital-art-culture-lab-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260810
SUMMARY:Deadline: G.MAP Digital Art Culture Lab 2026: Project Lab (Gwangju\
 , South Korea)
DESCRIPTION:The Gwangju Media Art Platform (G.MAP)\, an institution of Gwan
 gju\, UNESCO Creative City of Media Arts\, invites artists and teams worki
 ng in digital art to the 2026 Digital Art Culture Lab: Project Lab\, a fiv
 e-month creation support program (August to December 2026) covering the en
 tire creative process from idea development through production\, showcase 
 and archiving. WHO CAN APPLY: artists and groups in digital art wishing to
  create interdisciplinary projects using digital technology (all members a
 ged 19+\; international applicants welcome). Focus areas: digital art\, me
 dia art\, generative art\, AI\, interactive art\, XR\, AR\, VR\, interdisc
 iplinary\, art and technology. SELECTION: 14 teams total across two tracks
 : Track 1 Digital Transformation (7 artists/teams) and Track 2 Interdiscip
 linary Creation (7 teams). INTERNATIONAL ARTISTS CAN APPLY FOR TRACK 2 ONL
 Y. SUPPORT: creation and production grant up to KRW 30\,000\,000 per team 
 for Track 2\, two online mentoring sessions\, and a showcase in G.MAP exhi
 bition spaces and on large-scale public media platforms including the G.MA
 P LED Wall and city media facades. KEY DATES: application period 21 July t
 o 9 August 2026\, 6 PM KST\; results announced 14 August 2026\; program pe
 riod 20 August to 31 December 2026. Overseas participants (Track 2) must a
 ttend two in-person meetings in Jeonnam-Gwangju. HOW TO APPLY: submit the 
 application form and portfolio (PDF) by email to uartlabseoul@gmail.com. M
 ore info at urbanartlab.org/opencall. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Gwan
 gju Media Art Platform (G.MAP)\nLocation: Jeonnam-Gwangju\, South Korea. I
 nternational applicants welcome (Track 2 only).\nAward: Track 2 (Interdisc
 iplinary Creation): creation and production grant up to KRW 30\,000\,000 p
 er team\, plus accommodation\, equipment\, studio space\, production and t
 ravel support\, exhibition on G.MAP spaces and large-scale public media pl
 atforms (LED Wall\, city media facades)\, and publication. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: Five-month creation support program\, August to December 2026\n
 \nMore: https://urbanartlab.org/opencall/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://urbanartlab.org/opencall/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Jeonnam-Gwangju\, South Korea. International applicants welcome (T
 rack 2 only).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cannes-residence-cinema-de-demain-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: La Résidence du Festival de Cannes (Cinéma de Demain): 
 Screenwriting Residency in Paris
DESCRIPTION:La Résidence of the Festival de Cannes (part of Cinéma de Dem
 ain\, formerly La Cinéfondation) invites 12 directors each year to write 
 their screenplay in Paris. The programme provides a place of residence in 
 the heart of Paris\, a personalised programme accompanying the writing of 
 their scripts\, and a collective programme of forums with film industry pr
 ofessionals. The 12 directors from the two annual sessions present their p
 rojects at the CNC pavilion in Cannes\, giving 10-minute presentations to 
 an audience of professionals and a jury of three film industry personaliti
 es\; the CNC then awards a EUR 5\,000 grant to one director from each sess
 ion\, based on the project that most inspires the jury. The Cinef & Résid
 ence offices serve as a hub where directors meet and schedule private meet
 ings with potential producers. ELIGIBILITY: open to young filmmakers who h
 ave made one or several short films and no more than one feature film\; ca
 ndidates must NOT be residents or citizens of France. Selected filmmakers 
 must stay at the Résidence in Paris for the full session. SESSIONS: two p
 er year\, each lasting four and a half months\, the first running 15 March
  to 31 July and the second 1 October to 15 February. Artistic fields: film
 \, writing\, interdisciplinary. Deadline: 30 September 2026 at 16:00 UTC. 
 Free to apply.\n\nOrganisation: Festival de Cannes (Cinéma de Demain / La
  Résidence)\nLocation: Paris\, France. Candidates must not be residents o
 r citizens of France.\nAward: Fully supported: a place of residence in cen
 tral Paris\, a personalised script-writing programme\, and a collective pr
 ogramme of forums with film industry professionals. Directors present at t
 he CNC pavilion in Cannes\, where the CNC awards a EUR 5\,000 grant to one
  director per session. Free to apply.\nDuration: Four and a half months pe
 r session (two sessions a year)\n\nMore: https://cinemadedemain.festival-c
 annes.com/en/take-part/register-to-the-residence/?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://cinemadedemain.festival-cannes.com/en/take-part/register-to-the
 -residence/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Paris\, France. Candidates must not be residents or citizens of Fr
 ance.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fucina-futura-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Fucina Futura 2027 Residency (Marostica\, Italy)
DESCRIPTION:Fucina Futura is an artist residency in Marostica\, a small tow
 n about an hour from Venice\, offering a peaceful setting to develop resea
 rch in close contact with nature and the local community. The project span
 s two locations: the home designed by internationally renowned illustrator
  Elena Xausa near the historic centre\, and the atelier Fucina Ai Gorghi b
 y beloved local painter Daniele Marcon\, ideal for large-scale installatio
 ns. Led by visual artist Lavinia and curator Sofia (Futura Association)\, 
 the program invites residents to develop projects that engage with the com
 munity and the working location\, and encourages artists of any discipline
  to make full use of the large atelier. The central goal is the 'restituti
 on' to citizens\, fostering cultural exchange and community inclusion: thi
 s final phase is tailored to each artist and can take the form of an exhib
 ition\, performance\, workshop\, screening\, talk or informal sharing sess
 ion\, prioritising direct dialogue with the public and the contemporary re
 newal of local tradition. THREE residents are hosted together from 12 Apri
 l to 12 May 2027 and share a spacious house\; they receive a daily fee to 
 cover living expenses\, and travel expenses are also covered. Made possibl
 e by Culture Moves Europe. ELIGIBILITY: only artists residing in one of th
 e Creative Europe countries EXCEPT ITALY may apply. Creative Europe covers
  the 27 EU member states (other than Italy)\, plus Albania\, Armenia\, Bos
 nia and Herzegovina\, Georgia\, Iceland\, Kosovo\, Liechtenstein\, Moldova
 \, Montenegro\, North Macedonia\, Norway\, Serbia\, Tunisia and Ukraine\, 
 and includes the EU's Overseas Countries and Territories and Outermost Reg
 ions (Greenland\; French Guiana\, French Polynesia\, French Southern and A
 ntarctic Territories\, Guadeloupe\, Martinique\, Mayotte\, New Caledonia\,
  Reunion\, Saint Barthelemy\, Saint-Martin\, St. Pierre and Miquelon\, Wal
 lis and Futuna\; Azores\, Madeira\; Canary Islands\; Aruba\, Bonaire\, Cur
 acao\, Saba\, Sint Maarten\, Sint Eustatius). TIMELINE: apply via the Goog
 le Form by 20 August 2026\; selected artists informed by 30 August and mus
 t confirm participation by 8 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Futura Assoc
 iation (Fucina Futura)\, supported by Culture Moves Europe\nLocation: Maro
 stica\, Italy (one hour from Venice). Open to artists resident in a Creati
 ve Europe country except Italy.\nAward: Fully funded: daily fee to cover l
 iving expenses\, travel costs covered\, and shared house accommodation pro
 vided (supported by Culture Moves Europe). No application fee.\nDuration: 
 One month\, 12 April to 12 May 2027\; three residents hosted together\n\nM
 ore: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScwEmQ2EFrMoukgNorlOLE5kGvGr
 iMX0IdMBObmhfgRlSW-jQ/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScwEmQ2EFrMoukgNorlOLE5kGvGriM
 X0IdMBObmhfgRlSW-jQ/viewform?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Marostica\, Italy (one hour from Venice). Open to artists resident
  in a Creative Europe country except Italy.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:proac-jogos-eletronicos-05-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260727
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260728
SUMMARY:Deadline: ProAC / Fomento CultSP: Edital 05/2026\, Electronic Games
  Development and Publishing
DESCRIPTION:Sao Paulo state's ProAC scheme runs one of the largest dedicate
 d public funds for game development in Latin America. Edital 05/2026 split
 s BRL 6\,000\,000 across two modules of ten projects each: Module I for de
 veloping electronic games\, Module II for finalising and publishing them\,
  at BRL 300\,000 per project. At least six of the ten slots in each module
  are reserved for applicants based outside the state capital. ELIGIBILITY 
 IS RESTRICTED AND NARROW: individuals cannot apply. Applicants must be leg
 al entities\, meaning private companies (for-profit or not-for-profit) or 
 registered MEI microentrepreneurs\, headquartered in Sao Paulo state for a
 t least two years\, with artistic or cultural activity expressly stated in
  their founding documents. Applications close 27 July 2026 at 23:59:59 Bra
 silia time via fomento.sp.gov.br. Note this is a tight deadline.\n\nOrgani
 sation: Secretaria da Cultura\, Economia e Industria Criativas do Estado d
 e Sao Paulo (ProAC)\nLocation: Sao Paulo state\, Brazil. Open only to lega
 l entities headquartered in Sao Paulo state for at least two years.\nAward
 : BRL 300\,000 (around USD 55\,000 / EUR 50\,000) per selected project\, 2
 0 projects total across two modules\, from a BRL 6\,000\,000 pool. Registr
 ation is free.\nDuration: Module I funds game development\; Module II fund
 s finalisation and publishing of an existing game.\n\nMore: https://www.cu
 ltura.sp.gov.br/sec_cultura/Arquivo_de_Editais/Editais_Fomento_Cultsp/Fome
 nto_CultSP_2026/desenvolvimento_ou_finalizacao_e_publicacao_de_jogos_eletr
 onicos_2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.cultura.sp.gov.br/sec_cultura/Arquivo_de_Editais/Editais_Fo
 mento_Cultsp/Fomento_CultSP_2026/desenvolvimento_ou_finalizacao_e_publicac
 ao_de_jogos_eletronicos_2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
LOCATION:Sao Paulo state\, Brazil. Open only to legal entities headquartere
 d in Sao Paulo state for at least two years.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:india-foundation-arts-research-programme-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260810
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260811
SUMMARY:Deadline: India Foundation for the Arts: Arts Research Programme (M
 ay 2026 Request for Proposals)
DESCRIPTION:IFA's Arts Research Programme funds research into forms of know
 ing and thinking rooted in creative\, scholarly or cultural practice. It e
 xplicitly welcomes experimental and self-reflexive methodologies\, practic
 e-based inquiry\, interdisciplinary dialogue\, community engagement\, tran
 slation studies and work on marginalised narratives\, with a shareable pub
 lic outcome expected. Open to artists\, scholars\, curators\, translators\
 , educators and practitioner-researchers. ELIGIBILITY IS RESTRICTED: India
 n nationals only\, and PIO and OCI card holders are explicitly excluded\; 
 any collaborators must also be Indian nationals. Applicants need a PAN car
 d and valid ID. PROCESS NOTE: this is a two-stage email application\, not 
 a web form. Applicants were expected to discuss the project concept with t
 he Programme Officer (harshita@indiaifa.org) by 11 July 2026\, with the fi
 nal proposal\, CV and budget breakdown emailed by 10 August 2026 at 23:59 
 IST\, in an email no larger than 25 MB. Proposals may be written in any In
 dian language\, including English.\n\nOrganisation: India Foundation for t
 he Arts (IFA)\nLocation: India\; project-based with no residency requireme
 nt. Open to Indian nationals only.\nAward: Up to INR 500\,000 (around USD 
 5\,700 / EUR 4\,900) per project. Honorarium is capped at 35 percent of th
 e budget. Institutional overhead\, building costs\, infrastructure and equ
 ipment purchases are excluded. No application fee.\nDuration: Minimum 12 m
 onths\, maximum 18 months.\n\nMore: https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-
 may2026.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://indiaifa.org/whats-new/ar-rfp-may2026.html?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:India\; project-based with no residency requirement. Open to India
 n nationals only.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inlaks-fine-art-award-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inlaks Shivdasani Foundation: Inlaks Fine Art Award 2027
DESCRIPTION:A one-year award enabling an emerging Indian visual artist to p
 ursue independent practice\, with a stipulation to attend a recognised art
  residency programme in India for four weeks during the year. ELIGIBILITY 
 IS RESTRICTED: Indian citizens currently living in India\, born on or afte
 r 1 January 1996\, who are either recent graduates\, postgraduates or fina
 l-year students of a leading Indian visual arts institute\, or artists wit
 hout those formal credentials but with a record of exhibition participatio
 n. First-year MA students are not eligible. Awardees submit progress repor
 ts at six months and on completion\, plus documentation of the residency. 
 Applications close 15 August 2026 at 23:59 IST via Submittable. Shortlisti
 ng is expected in late October 2026 with interviews in early November.\n\n
 Organisation: Inlaks Shivdasani Foundation\nLocation: India. Open to India
 n citizens currently living in India.\nAward: INR 300\,000 (around USD 3\,
 400 / EUR 2\,950) for one year\, plus a fully covered four-week art reside
 ncy at an Indian art institution. No application fee.\nDuration: Twelve mo
 nths of independent practice\, including a mandatory four-week residency w
 ithin India.\n\nMore: https://inlaksfoundation.submittable.com/submit/75e1
 4093-df9b-41c5-af96-ce2360e64e1b/the-inlaks-fine-art-award-2027?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://inlaksfoundation.submittable.com/submit/75e14093-df9b-41c5-af96
 -ce2360e64e1b/the-inlaks-fine-art-award-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:India. Open to Indian citizens currently living in India.
END:VEVENT
