BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uk-global-screen-fund-video-game-release-2026@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: UK Global Screen Fund: International Distribution\, Video
  Game Release Funding
DESCRIPTION:The newest strand of the UK Global Screen Fund extends the fund
 's international-distribution logic from film into UK independent narrativ
 e video games. It supports marketing and release activity for games with d
 emonstrable international appeal and sales potential\, awarding non-recoup
 able grants to help UK studios reach overseas players. ELIGIBILITY: applic
 ations must be made by the UK-based development studio deemed the lead dev
 eloper for the game\, and only narrative video games qualify. One applicat
 ion per game. The wider UKGSF requirement that applicants be independent s
 creen-sector companies registered and centrally managed in the UK applies.
  TIMING: the round opened 14 May 2026 and closes at 12pm on Friday 14 Augu
 st 2026\, making it one of the few hard-deadline BFI opportunities current
 ly live. Separately\, applications must be filed at least eight weeks befo
 re the funded activity starts\, so a game releasing in early autumn may fa
 ll outside the window even if the application lands before 14 August. NOTE
 : award amounts are not published on the fund page\, so treat the range as
  unknown rather than estimated. A cumulative cap of GBP 625\,000 per organ
 isation across all UK Global Screen Fund strands over any three-year perio
 d also applies.\n\nOrganisation: British Film Institute (UK Global Screen 
 Fund\, backed by DCMS)\nLocation: United Kingdom (applications from the UK
 -based lead development studio)\nAward: Non-recoupable grant towards inter
 national marketing and release activity\; the award range is not published
  on the fund page. A cap of GBP 625\,000 applies across all UKGSF strands 
 per organisation over any three-year period. No application fee.\nDuration
 : Single release-activity award\; apply no later than eight weeks before t
 he funded activity begins.\n\nMore: https://www.bfi.org.uk/get-funding-sup
 port/funding-support-international-activity/uk-global-screen-fund/uk-globa
 l-screen-fund-ukgsf-international-distribution-video-game-release-funding?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.bfi.org.uk/get-funding-support/funding-support-internationa
 l-activity/uk-global-screen-fund/uk-global-screen-fund-ukgsf-international
 -distribution-video-game-release-funding?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom (applications from the UK-based lead development st
 udio)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bfi-national-lottery-creative-challenge-fund-round-2-2026@artificialnou
 veau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260921
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260922
SUMMARY:Deadline: BFI National Lottery Creative Challenge Fund (Round Two)
DESCRIPTION:The Creative Challenge Fund does not fund films directly. It fu
 nds the organisations that run project-development programmes for UK creat
 ives working on narrative\, documentary or animation features\, or on narr
 ative immersive media projects. The BFI's stated aim is to decentralise de
 velopment away from a small number of London-centric gatekeepers and build
  a wider\, more resilient ecosystem of talent-development providers across
  the UK\, with a recently added emphasis on independent producers. ELIGIBI
 LITY: only legally constituted UK-based organisations may apply. Individua
 l filmmakers seeking development money should look to the BFI Filmmaking F
 und's Development strand instead. TIMING: Round One closed in March 2026 a
 nd has made its first awards. Round Two closes Monday 21 September 2026\, 
 with funded programme activity required to begin between 1 January and 30 
 June 2027. NOTE: award amounts are not published on the fund page. Success
 ful applicants must prominently credit BFI National Lottery support.\n\nOr
 ganisation: British Film Institute (BFI National Lottery)\nLocation: Unite
 d Kingdom (UK-based organisations running development programmes for UK cr
 eatives)\nAward: Award amounts are not published on the fund page. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: Funded programme activity must start between 1 Jan
 uary and 30 June 2027 and\, in most cases\, be delivered within 12 months 
 unless the applicant is running multiple editions.\n\nMore: https://www.bf
 i.org.uk/get-funding-support/create-films-tv-or-new-formats-storytelling/b
 fi-national-lottery-creative-challenge-fund?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.bfi.org.uk/get-funding-support/create-films-tv-or-new-forma
 ts-storytelling/bfi-national-lottery-creative-challenge-fund?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United Kingdom (UK-based organisations running development program
 mes for UK creatives)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:project-liberty-co-lab-fund-round-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260729
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260730
SUMMARY:Deadline: Project Liberty: Co-Lab Fund Micro-Grants (Round 2\, 2026
 )
DESCRIPTION:MEMBERS ONLY: this is a member-led collaboration fund\, not an 
 open public call. Projects must be led by a Project Liberty Coalition (All
 iance) member organization. Round 2 shifts to micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, an iterative change from Round 1 intended to spread support acro
 ss more of the Coalition\; organizations that did not receive a Round 1 aw
 ard are preferred. SCOPE: micro-grants fund two kinds of work\, (1) discre
 te projects that strengthen the Coalition itself\, such as Shared Services
  or convenings\, and (2) time-sensitive collaborative projects within a de
 signated workstream. In both cases the work should begin quickly and produ
 ce something concrete. A STRONG APPLICATION SHOWS: work that builds the Co
 alition as an entity\, such as shared tools\, infrastructure or connection
 s that make joint action easier\; clear alignment with a Coalition workstr
 eam\; value for multiple Coalition organizations\; a strong case that the 
 work would not happen\, or would move more slowly\, without the Fund\; a c
 oncrete outcome such as a tool\, resource\, convening\, campaign or resear
 ch product\; and readiness to begin substantive work within two weeks of a
 pproval. LIMIT: one application per organization\, submitted through the e
 xisting Co-Lab Fund application form. Round 1 results were still pending a
 t the time of this call\, with funded projects to be shared soon and engag
 ement opportunities expected across the work. Prospective applicants unsur
 e whether an idea fits this round are invited to reach out first. DEADLINE
 : 29 July 2026 at midnight. Background on the Co-Lab program is in the Coa
 lition's overview document.\n\nOrganisation: Project Liberty (Alliance Ste
 erCo)\; pooled fund supported by Future of Life Institute\, Omidyar Networ
 k\, Ergo Impact\, and Project Liberty\nLocation: Members of the Project Li
 berty coalition (Alliance). Projects led by a Coalition member organizatio
 n\; geography otherwise unrestricted.\nAward: Micro-grants of USD 10\,000 
 or less\, a deliberate change from Round 1's larger awards so the Fund rea
 ches more of the Coalition. Preference given to organizations that did not
  receive a Round 1 award. No application fee.\nDuration: Short\, discrete 
 projects. Applicants must be ready to begin substantive work within two we
 eks of approval. This is the final round of the summer\; the Fund is expec
 ted to resume later in 2026.\n\nMore: https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTk
 NJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://forms.fillout.com/t/6AGEGhTkNJus?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Members of the Project Liberty coalition (Alliance). Projects led 
 by a Coalition member organization\; geography otherwise unrestricted.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:antikythera-agentworld-journal-cfp-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: Antikythera Journal: Agentworld Special Issue\, Call for 
 Papers
DESCRIPTION:Antikythera\, a research programme on planetary computation and
  the evolution of intelligence\, invites contributions to Agentworld\, a s
 pecial issue of its peer-reviewed MIT Press journal launching Fall 2026. T
 HEME: Agentworld is a research initiative on the medium-term future of hyb
 rid human-AI societies\, framed not around tools for agentic task completi
 on but around what the brief calls 'the human perplexity of deep agentic d
 iffusion'. It treats human societies as the evolutionary niches into which
  new artificial species must adapt\, where the constraints of agentic inte
 gration are qualitative and open-ended (psychological\, philosophical\, li
 nguistic\, symbolic\, existential) rather than utility functions to be sol
 ved. The framing describes a 'parasociety' of densely reticulated social g
 raphs in which agent-to-agent communication constitutes most socially sign
 ificant interaction\, humans and agents anthropomorphise each other by inh
 eritance rather than necessity\, and the two overlapping societies act as 
 mutual evolutionary niches. The disciplinary posture proposed is 'a preemp
 tive anthropology of open-world centaur societies': building the vocabular
 y\, scenarios and conceptual armature for an emergent sociality while it i
 s still forming\, rather than describing it after the fact. WHAT THEY WANT
 : theoretical essays and technical papers that engage the Agentworld theme
 s directly and explain how the contribution adds to the special issue's co
 re questions. Philosophical\, theoretical\, technical\, design-led and pro
 duct-oriented research are all in scope. The call explicitly favours work 
 that exceeds conventional academic journal formats\, including contributio
 ns combining philosophical argument\, technical analysis\, design research
 \, diagrams\, software\, infrastructure proposals\, visual media or web-na
 tive formats. Contributors may optionally propose still images\, diagrams\
 , video\, web-based or interactive components\, and should say whether the
 y have the design\, development or production capacity to realise them\; t
 hese elements are not required. SUBMIT: an abstract or proposal of up to 5
 00 words\; author name(s)\, affiliation(s)\, contact details\, biography(i
 es) and CV(s)\; a designated lead author or contact person for collaborati
 ve submissions\; and a portfolio with at least one relevant work sample su
 ch as a preprint\, published article or paper. Full paper drafts are encou
 raged where available but not required at the initial stage. Submissions m
 ay be individual or collaborative. TIMING: the review window opened 25 Jun
 e 2026. NOTE: no closing date is published on the call page\; the deadline
  shown here is a placeholder set roughly a month out\, so check the site o
 r submit early.\n\nOrganisation: Antikythera (Antikythera: Journal for the
  Philosophy of Planetary Computation\, published with MIT Press)\nLocation
 : Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-forward)
 \nAward: Unpaid. No honorarium or fee is offered for accepted contribution
 s\; the return is peer-reviewed publication in the MIT Press-published Ant
 ikythera Journal. No submission fee.\nDuration: Single publication\; first
  selection of articles published Fall 2026\n\nMore: https://agentworld.ant
 ikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral#ca
 ll-for-papers
URL:https://agentworld.antikythera.org/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral#call-for-papers
LOCATION:Remote (online submission\; the journal is web-first and design-fo
 rward)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:european-ai-society-fund-building-narrative-power-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Building Narrative Power: EUR 2m funding and learning pro
 gramme
DESCRIPTION:The European AI & Society Fund is launching Building Narrative 
 Power\, a EUR 2 million funding and learning programme to counter Big Tech
 's dominance over how AI is understood\, and to help people and communitie
 s shape new ways of thinking and talking about the technology they want. T
 he Fund's strategy is to build the power of the public interest community 
 to shape the direction of AI in Europe\; narratives are central to creatin
 g the conditions for a greater say over how AI is adopted and used. THREE 
 THEMES where research shows the public already feels strongly: (1) DATA CE
 NTRES: as the EU aims to triple data centre numbers in the next five to se
 ven years\, pollution\, energy prices and compromised grid access have mob
 ilised communities across the political spectrum\; proposals should explor
 e narrative questions that move communities beyond resistance towards enga
 gement in shaping data centre expansion. (2) AI AND JOBS: 57% of the publi
 c in a recent study expressed concern about how AI will change employment\
 , more pronounced among lower-income people\, and the concern is not only 
 displacement but the quality and nature of work\, autonomy and dignity\; p
 roposals should move the debate from generalised anxiety into a mobilising
  issue that shapes the future of work. (3) YOUNG PEOPLE: beyond the stride
 nt social media ban debate\, young people encounter AI in education\, frie
 ndships and future careers\, and their wider views are rarely heard\; prop
 osals should build narratives that help young people shape the technologie
 s that will influence their lives. LEARNING PROGRAMME: grantees join a com
 munity of practice\, sharing experiences across issues and places\, suppor
 ting each other through delivery and reflecting on which strategies and st
 ories have most salience\, with findings feeding back to the Fund and othe
 r funders. ABOUT THE FUNDER: the European AI & Society Fund is a pooled fu
 nd with 18 funding partners that mobilises a public interest community in 
 Europe to fight for AI that serves people\, society and planet\; it takes 
 no EU\, government or Big Tech money. ELIGIBILITY: this call is only open 
 to organisations in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, the Republic 
 of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Gre
 ece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithu
 ania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, the Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\,
  Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden and Switzerland\; applicat
 ions from the UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries ar
 e not accepted. Guidance for applicants and a full application-form previe
 w are published so you can prepare a draft before submitting. HOW TO APPLY
 : create an account on the Fund's application platform Optimy (link in the
  guidance documents) and submit the first-stage application before 31 Augu
 st 2026. KEY DATES: call launched 16 July 2026\; information sessions 22 J
 uly\, 29 July and 24 August 2026 (11:00 to 12:00 CET)\; first-stage deadli
 ne 31 August 2026\; unsuccessful applicants notified 16 October 2026\; sho
 rtlisted applicants invited to submit a detailed proposal on 19 October 20
 26\, due 16 November 2026\; funding decisions notified 14 December 2026\; 
 grants start February 2027. Questions to info@europeanaifund.org\; an FAQ 
 covering eligibility\, coalitions\, non-profit status and what the grant c
 an be spent on is published at the bottom of the programme page.\n\nOrgani
 sation: European AI & Society Fund\nLocation: Organisations registered in 
 Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia\, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, De
 nmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, Germany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\
 , Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtenstein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malt
 a\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Portugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Sloven
 ia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. The UK\, the Western Balkans and other
  non-European countries are not eligible for this programme.\nAward: Grant
 s of up to EUR 150\,000 from a EUR 2 million programme\, plus a place in t
 he accompanying learning programme and community of practice. Payments are
  usually 50% on grant signing\, 40% midway through the grant period subjec
 t to previous funds having been spent\, and 10% on completion. As a genera
 l rule organisations should not request more than their previous annual bu
 dget unless the need is motivated and demonstrated\; without a previous an
 nual budget\, request an amount proportional to the project. No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: Project grant with an accompanying learning programme\; 
 grants start February 2027\n\nMore: https://europeanaifund.org/newspublica
 tions/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-building-narrative-power-funding-and-lear
 ning-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://europeanaifund.org/newspublications/call-for-proposals-new-e2m-
 building-narrative-power-funding-and-learning-programme/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Organisations registered in Austria\, Belgium\, Bulgaria\, Croatia
 \, Republic of Cyprus\, Czechia\, Denmark\, Estonia\, Finland\, France\, G
 ermany\, Greece\, Hungary\, Iceland\, Ireland\, Italy\, Latvia\, Liechtens
 tein\, Lithuania\, Luxembourg\, Malta\, Netherlands\, Norway\, Poland\, Po
 rtugal\, Romania\, Slovakia\, Slovenia\, Spain\, Sweden or Switzerland. Th
 e UK\, the Western Balkans and other non-European countries are not eligib
 le for this programme.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:lse-digipulse-research-officer-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260722
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260723
SUMMARY:Deadline: LSE Research Officer: DigiPulse\, Digital Futures for Chi
 ldren Centre
DESCRIPTION:DigiPulse is a three-year longitudinal\, international research
  project in LSE's Digital Futures for Children centre investigating the re
 al-time relationships between children's smartphone use and their wellbein
 g\, with data collected in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. The project combines
  passive smartphone tracking\, Ecological Momentary Assessment (EMA) and l
 ongitudinal surveys with around 750 children and young people aged 10-17. 
 THE ROLE: the Research Officer plays a central part in project management\
 , development\, data collection and analysis\, with particular responsibil
 ity for developing and refining EMA instruments\, contributing to the rese
 arch literature and hypotheses\, supporting participatory and consultative
  work with children\, and conducting statistical analysis and producing fi
 ndings\, working closely with partners in the UK\, Brazil and Kenya. REQUI
 REMENTS: a completed PhD in a relevant discipline (e.g. media and communic
 ations\, psychology\, psychiatry\, sociology\, education\, or computationa
 l/data social science)\; experience developing surveys or questionnaire in
 struments\; experience designing or conducting longitudinal research studi
 es\; and experience with EMA\, experience-sampling or other intensive long
 itudinal data collection methods. APPLY via jobs.lse.ac.uk\; questions to 
 k.g.ringmar-sylwander@lse.ac.uk. Closing date 22 July 2026 (23:59 UK time)
 \; late applications are not accepted.\n\nOrganisation: London School of E
 conomics and Political Science (LSE)\, Department of Media and Communicati
 ons\nLocation: London\, UK (hybrid working)\nAward: GBP 43\,277 to 51\,714
  per year inclusive of London allowance\, with potential to progress to GB
 P 55\,497. Full-time (1.0 FTE)\, funded until December 2028. Benefits: occ
 upational pension scheme\, generous annual leave\, hybrid working\, traini
 ng and development. No application fee.\nDuration: Full-time fixed-term po
 st\, funded until December 2028\n\nMore: https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/
 W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer-digipulse-digital-futures-for-child
 ren-centre/Referral?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refer
 ral
URL:https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/2504/0/471333/15539/research-officer
 -digipulse-digital-futures-for-children-centre/Referral?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (hybrid working)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:govai-research-fellow-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260816
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260817
SUMMARY:Deadline: GovAI Research Fellows and Research Scholars (AI Governan
 ce) 2026
DESCRIPTION:GovAI is hiring for two research roles with a single shared app
 lication (apply once to be considered for both). RESEARCH FELLOWS play a c
 entral role in GovAI's research: experienced researchers who conduct indep
 endent\, high-quality work to inform high-stakes decisions in government a
 nd industry\, whether through policy memos\, blog posts\, academic publica
 tions or strategic advising\, alongside mentoring early-career researchers
  (such as Summer and Winter Fellows) and helping shape GovAI's research ag
 enda. Two-year contract with the possibility of renewal\; Fellows work und
 er a Senior Research Fellow or leadership-team member but have substantial
  autonomy over projects\, agendas and publication formats. RESEARCH SCHOLA
 RS join for a one-year visiting position designed to support the career de
 velopment of AI governance researchers and practitioners: conducting polic
 y\, social science or technical research\, advising policymakers\, or star
 ting new applied projects. TOPICS include risk management\, threat modelin
 g\, economics of AI\, geopolitics of AI\, public policy and technical gove
 rnance\; candidates working on adjacent topics are also welcome. LOCATION:
  London or Washington DC preferred\, but open to candidates based elsewher
 e (for example the Bay Area). ELIGIBILITY: open to a wide range of academi
 c and professional backgrounds (computer science\, political science\, pub
 lic policy\, economics\, philosophy\, law\, plus government/industry/civil
 -society experience)\; no strict educational requirement\, but the stronge
 st Fellow candidates typically have multiple years of relevant research or
  policy experience and a track record of high-quality\, impactful work. PR
 OCESS: written application\; a paid remote work test (some workstreams add
  a research agenda and a second paid test)\; then a final stage of two int
 erviews and reference checks. Deep expertise in technical governance\, fro
 ntier safety design\, cybersecurity or US policy is especially welcome. De
 adline 23:59 Anywhere on Earth\, Sunday 16 August 2026. Contact recruitmen
 t@governance.ai with location or access questions.\n\nOrganisation: Centre
  for the Governance of AI (GovAI)\nLocation: London\, UK or Washington\, D
 C preferred\; open to candidates based elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship avai
 lable\; US sponsorship only in exceptional cases.\nAward: Salary by experi
 ence\, role and location. Research Fellow: London GBP 84\,000-103\,500\; W
 ashington DC USD 134\,500-170\,000. Across both roles the combined range s
 tarts at GBP 75\,000 (London) / USD 100\,000 (DC)\, with the lower bounds 
 applying to the Research Scholar position. Plus pension or 401(k) contribu
 tion and benefits (health\, dental and vision insurance\; annual wellbeing
  and professional-development budgets\; coworking allowance for remote sta
 ff\; flexible hours\; extended parental leave\; 33 days paid vacation incl
 uding public holidays). No application fee.\nDuration: Research Fellow: 2-
 year full-time contract\, renewable. Research Scholar: 1-year visiting pos
 ition.\n\nMore: https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.governance.ai/opportunities/research-fellow?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK or Washington\, DC preferred\; open to candidates base
 d elsewhere. UK visa sponsorship available\; US sponsorship only in except
 ional cases.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bloomberg-green-docs-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260815
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bloomberg Green Docs Competition 2026 (Short Climate Docu
 mentary)
DESCRIPTION:The Bloomberg Green Docs competition is open to all eligible fi
 lmmakers competing for a $25\,000 grand prize for a short climate document
 ary. The call seeks films that explore our climate future and reveal the w
 orld we are making today: ecosystems being restored\, tomorrow's zero-carb
 on communities being formed\, and habitat extremes warning of cascading cl
 imate consequences. REQUIREMENTS: films must address the urgency of climat
 e change and run under 10 minutes including credits. The jury awards a $25
 \,000 grand prize\, announced at the Bloomberg Green Docs Film Festival in
  New York City on 22 September 2026\, where the top five documentaries wil
 l be screened. Check the official rules on the competition site for eligib
 ility details. DEADLINE: submissions accepted through 14 August 2026.\n\nO
 rganisation: Bloomberg Green\nLocation: Open call for filmmakers (see elig
 ibility rules on the site)\; festival screening in New York City\, USA.\nA
 ward: $25\,000 grand prize awarded by a jury\; the top five documentaries 
 screen at the Bloomberg Green Docs Film Festival in New York City. No entr
 y fee stated.\nDuration: Winner announced at the festival on 22 September 
 2026\n\nMore: https://www.bloomberg.com/greendocs/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.bloomberg.com/greendocs/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantd
 esk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open call for filmmakers (see eligibility rules on the site)\; fes
 tival screening in New York City\, USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arm-holding-commission-h-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260725
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260726
SUMMARY:Deadline: A.R.M. Holding / Art Dubai Projects: H Residence Sculptur
 e Park Commission (Dubai)
DESCRIPTION:A.R.M. Holding and HUNA\, with the commission supported and man
 aged by Art Dubai Projects\, invite artists to propose a permanent public 
 artwork for the H Residence Sculpture Park in Dubai\, the anchor piece of 
 the park's programme. Artists respond to the theme 'We Emerge Stronger tha
 n Before' through a permanent\, site-specific outdoor sculpture\; the stro
 ngest proposals will find what the phrase opens up (continuity\, endurance
 \, renewal\, collective presence\, the long view) through material\, form\
 , scale and the conditions of the site. Where language or text is incorpor
 ated\, Arabic should lead. TECHNICAL: footprint minimum around 15 sqm\, up
  to 3 m high\; weight restriction 5.5 kN/m2\; must be suitable for long-te
 rm outdoor display in Dubai (summer over 40 C\, humidity to 90 percent\, a
 bout six hours direct sun daily)\; presence by day and night. ELIGIBILITY:
  open to artists with or without gallery representation\; UAE-based\, regi
 onal and international artists welcome\; prior experience with permanent o
 r large-scale outdoor/public installation required. BUDGET: AED 1\,000\,00
 0 all-inclusive\; shortlisted artists receive full budget parameters and p
 roposal fees at the proposal development stage. SUBMISSION: CV and portfol
 io (attention to outdoor\, large-scale or permanent commissions)\, concept
  statement (max 300 words)\, a render or visualisation in context plus at 
 least one sketch\, technical specifications\, and a site response. TIMELIN
 E: submissions close 25 July 2026\; shortlist notified from 10 August\; co
 mmission awarded after September proposal development\; work unveiled at H
  Residence on 2 December 2026. Local fabrication strongly preferred. Quest
 ions: Shahad@artdubai.ae.\n\nOrganisation: A.R.M. Holding / HUNA / Art Dub
 ai Projects\nLocation: H Residence Sculpture Park\, Dubai\, UAE. Open to U
 AE-based\, regional and international artists.\nAward: AED 1\,000\,000 com
 mission envelope\, inclusive of artist fee\, design development\, fabricat
 ion\, materials\, production\, transport\, installation\, lighting and fin
 ishing. Proposal fees are available for shortlisted artists. No submission
  fee.\nDuration: Proposal development September 2026\; fabrication autumn 
 2026\; unveiling 2 December 2026\n\nMore: https://www.artdubai.ae/wp-conte
 nt/uploads/2026/06/ADP_ARM-HOLDING-COMMISSION_OPEN-CALL-PACK-.pdf?utm_sour
 ce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
