UID:prohelvetia-reflection-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Reflection (All Disciplines)
DESCRIPTION:Reflection is a Pro Helvetia grant supporting collective mediat
 ion projects that use innovative formats to foster critical reflection on 
 artistic practice and cultural transformation\, across all supported disci
 plines (design\, literature\, music\, performing arts\, visual arts\, and 
 innovation and society). Open to collectives comprising at least two cultu
 ral practitioners (artists\, creatives\, cultural practitioners\, or art e
 ducators)\; solo projects are excluded. ELIGIBILITY: requires a connection
  to Switzerland (Swiss citizenship\, or permanent residency with regular a
 rtistic activity in Switzerland)\; not open to practitioners without a Swi
 ss connection. For international collaborations\, at least 50 percent of p
 ractitioners must be from Switzerland. AMOUNT: maximum CHF 25\,000\, and n
 o more than 50 percent of the total budget. Deadlines twice a year: 1 Marc
 h (23:59 CET) and 1 September (23:59 CEST). Apply via the Pro Helvetia por
 tal.\n\nOrganisation: Pro Helvetia (Swiss Arts Council)\nLocation: Collect
 ives of at least two cultural practitioners\; for international collaborat
 ions at least 50 percent must be from Switzerland.\nAward: Maximum CHF 25\
 ,000 (cannot exceed 50 percent of total budget). No application fee.\nDura
 tion: Collective mediation project fostering critical reflection\; deadlin
 es twice yearly (1 March and 1 September).\n\nMore: https://prohelvetia.ch
 /en/find-support/reflection?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://prohelvetia.ch/en/find-support/reflection?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Collectives of at least two cultural practitioners\; for internati
 onal collaborations at least 50 percent must be from Switzerland.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:hibrides-biennial-artistic-research-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: Híbrides: Small Embodied Data - Call for Contributions (
 2nd Edition)
DESCRIPTION:Híbrides is a biennial of artistic research co-organised by th
 e Axolot.cat collective and the DARTS (Design\, Art\, Technology and Socie
 ty) research group at the UOC in Barcelona. The second edition\, "Small Em
 bodied Data\," centres on the concept of classification -- approached thro
 ugh machine learning\, data science\, algorithmic systems\, and questions 
 of who designs classifiers\, under what ethical codes\, and to what end. T
 he biennial brings classification into dialogue with its previous themes o
 f Small Data (community-defined\, intentional data collection and analysis
 ) and Embodiment (bodily experience as a foundation of situated\, sensory 
 knowledge). Both academic and non-academic contributions are welcome. FORM
 ATS ACCEPTED: Oral Presentations (20 minutes\; artistic practice\, artisti
 c research\, or academic research projects\; may be video-recorded for a p
 ublic archive and developed into short papers)\; Performances (20-30 minut
 es\; must engage with the physical body\, stage\, and technology\; complet
 e works\, including previously presented work\; takes place in a multipurp
 ose space with technical support). SUBMISSION REQUIREMENTS: online form wi
 th abstract (max 1\,000 characters)\, professional biography (max 1\,000 c
 haracters)\, and link to representative materials (3-5 images\; video and 
 technical rider required for performances). HONORARIA: oral presentations 
 €100\; performances €400\; invoice required in both cases. Participant
 s must follow the event's code of conduct. Event runs 26-28 November 2026 
 at UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agustí\, Barcelona. Deadline: 
 7 September 2026. Submit at https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html.\n\n
 Organisation: Axolot.cat collective and DARTS research group\, Universitat
  Oberta de Catalunya (UOC)\nLocation: Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou cam
 pus and Convent de Sant Agustí. In-person attendance required.\nAward: Or
 al presentations: €100 honorarium (invoice required). Performances: €4
 00 honorarium (invoice required). No submission fee.\nDuration: Festival r
 uns 26-28 November 2026.\n\nMore: https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.htm
 l?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://hibrides.axolot.cat/opencall.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaug
 rantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Barcelona\, Spain\; UOC Poblenou campus and Convent de Sant Agust
 í. In-person attendance required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wheeler-centre-next-chapter-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Wheeler Centre: The Next Chapter 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Next Chapter is The Wheeler Centre's flagship writing progr
 am\, designed to elevate Australian stories that are underrepresented and 
 to nurture a new wave of writers to tell them. Each fellow receives a AUD 
 $15\,000 stipend and is paired with a handpicked mentor for a twelve-month
  period to progress a major writing project. Fellows also receive a reside
 ncy at Varuna\, The National Writers' House\; an end-of-year publishing in
 tensive at The Wheeler Centre\; and meaningful opportunities to connect wi
 th peers\, agents\, publishers\, and readers. One fellowship place is dedi
 cated specifically to a poet: The Malcolm Robertson Fellowship. 2027 judge
 s: Melissa Lucashenko\, Micaela Sahhar\, Cher Tan\, and Melanie Saward. EL
 IGIBILITY: Australian writers. No specific genre restrictions stated\; the
  program focuses on stories that are not currently being told and voices t
 hat are underrepresented. TIMELINE: submissions open 2 June 2026\; submiss
 ions close 31 July 2026 (5pm)\; 2027 Next Chapter Fellows announced 10 Nov
 ember 2026. Questions: thenextchapter@wheelercentre.com. Apply at https://
 www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter.\n\nOrganisation: The Wheeler Centr
 e\nLocation: Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Na
 tional Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Whe
 eler Centre.\nAward: AUD $15\,000 stipend per fellow. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months.\n\nMore: https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chap
 ter?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Nationa
 l Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Wheeler 
 Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:richell-prize-emerging-writers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers\, presented by the Emerg
 ing Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\, is an annual prize for unpu
 blished writers of adult fiction and adult narrative non-fiction. The winn
 er receives AUD $10\,000 and a 12-month mentorship with a Hachette Austral
 ia publisher\, who will work with the writer to develop their manuscript\,
  with first option to consider the finished work for publication. Long- an
 d short-listed writers are also eligible to apply for a Casa Morphosi Resi
 dency in Abruzzo\, Italy. The prize was established in memory of Hachette 
 Australia CEO Matt Richell (d. 2014). ELIGIBILITY: must currently reside i
 n Australia (Australian citizens\, permanent residents\, and long-term vis
 a holders residing in Australia\; those residing overseas are not eligible
 )\; unpublished writers only -- no previous publication by a publishing ho
 use (including small press or independent publishers in any genre)\; publi
 cation in anthologies\, literary journals\, scholarly works\, writing PhD 
 projects\, or self-publishing does not disqualify. ELIGIBLE GENRES: adult 
 fiction (including short story collections) and adult narrative non-fictio
 n (including memoir and essay collections). Poetry\, scripts\, plays\, pic
 ture books\, practical non-fiction\, and works for children or young adult
 s are not eligible. SUBMISSION FORMAT: single PDF comprising the first thr
 ee chapters (maximum 20\,000 words)\, a one-page synopsis\, and a chapter 
 breakdown of up to two A4 pages\; plus a statement (up to 750 words) on ho
 w winning the prize will further your writing career\, entered in the onli
 ne form. Manuscript must be complete or intended to be completed\; does no
 t need to be finished at time of submission. A4\, double-spaced\, 12pt Rom
 an font (e.g. Garamond or Times New Roman)\, 3cm margins\, page numbers. T
 IMELINE: prize opened 23 April 2026\; closes 13 July 2026 at 11:59pm AEST\
 ; longlist announced 11 September 2026\; shortlist 16 October 2026\; winne
 r announced 6 November 2026. Administered with support from Simpsons Solic
 itors. Apply at https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/
 .\n\nOrganisation: Emerging Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\nLoca
 tion: Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.\nAward: AUD $10\,000
  prize money (donated by Hachette Australia)\; 12-month mentorship with a 
 Hachette Australia publisher\; first option to consider finished work for 
 publication. Long- and short-listed writers also eligible to apply for a C
 asa Morphosi Residency in Abruzzo\, Italy. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 12-month mentorship.\n\nMore: https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/riche
 ll-prize-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:adk-ems-berlin-stockholm-residency-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: AdK / EMS Berlin-Stockholm Bilateral Residency Program 20
 26-27
DESCRIPTION:A new bilateral residency collaboration between the Studio für
  Elektroakustische Musik at the Akademie der Künste in Berlin and Elektro
 nmusikstudion EMS in Stockholm. Four artists will be selected to develop a
 nd present new works in both cities\, with presentations at KONTAKTE 2027 
 in Berlin and at a corresponding event in Stockholm. The program supports 
 new artistic and technical initiatives at the intersection of sound\, musi
 c\, and technology in the field of time-based\, performative\, and digital
  arts. ELIGIBLE CATEGORIES: (1) new instrumental and live-electronic works
 \; (2) acousmatic music\, computer music\, radio art\, and other forms of 
 electroacoustic music and sound art\; (3) projects from artists of any dis
 cipline for whom sound is a central element\; (4) curatorial\, publishing\
 , and educational projects in sound-based art practice\, particularly thos
 e relating in content to the AdK Studio or EMS\; (5) independent research 
 in acoustics\, computer science\, or musicology -- specifically software d
 evelopment\, spatialization\, or network technologies. Open to individuals
  and groups. Project duration is flexible and arranged by agreement. SUPPO
 RT: travel and accommodation costs for Berlin and Stockholm covered\; pres
 entation fees paid for both venues. Selection is made by the director and 
 advisory board of the Studio für Elektroakustische Musik and the director
  of EMS\; decision is final with no legal entitlement to funding. Incomple
 te or late applications are not considered. This call supplements the Stud
 io's regular residency program (separate deadline June 30\, 2026 for July-
 December 2026 period). Apply exclusively via the online form at https://nc
 .adk.de/index.php/apps/forms/s/HD2nxbzqwgeMHWN6xHg9bQrA. Questions: estudi
 o@adk.de. Deadline: 31 July 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Studio für Elektroakus
 tische Musik\, Akademie der Künste (Berlin) and Elektronmusikstudion EMS 
 (Stockholm)\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany and Stockholm\, Sweden\; travel an
 d accommodation covered for stays in both cities.\nAward: Travel and accom
 modation costs for stays in Berlin and Stockholm covered\; fees paid for p
 resentation at KONTAKTE Festival 2027 (Berlin) and for presentation in Sto
 ckholm. Amount not publicly stated. No application fee.\nDuration: Flexibl
 e\, by agreement\; works to be presented at KONTAKTE 2027 in Berlin and at
  a Stockholm presentation.\n\nMore: https://nc.adk.de/index.php/apps/forms
 /s/HD2nxbzqwgeMHWN6xHg9bQrA?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://nc.adk.de/index.php/apps/forms/s/HD2nxbzqwgeMHWN6xHg9bQrA?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany and Stockholm\, Sweden\; travel and accommodation
  covered for stays in both cities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:democracy-stories-lab-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260914
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260915
SUMMARY:Deadline: Democracy Stories Lab: Open Call for Visual Storytellers
DESCRIPTION:The Democracy Stories Lab\, an initiative of People Powered (a 
 global hub for participatory democracy) in partnership with the Democracy 
 Narratives Alliance\, invites creators worldwide to submit visual content 
 -- short videos\, illustrations\, or graphics -- that tells a compelling s
 tory about democracy\, voice\, fairness\, belonging\, and community power.
  Content can be original or already published\, as long as it is the appli
 cant's own work and engages with narrative frameworks from the Democracy N
 arratives Alliance's research on 'How to Talk About Democracy.' Tone is op
 en\; serious and comedic approaches are both welcome. SUBMISSIONS: up to t
 wo finished visual content pieces per applicant\, answering the prompt of 
 what the future would look like if people had a real say in the decisions 
 that shape their lives. FORMATS: primarily visual social media content\, i
 ncluding short videos (under 3 minutes\, vertically oriented) and illustra
 tions\; written or audio pieces are accepted if presented in a visual form
 at\, for example as a filmed performance. Each submission should build on 
 one of five narrative types from the DNA research brief 'How to Talk About
  Democracy': outcome-oriented (how democracy shows results)\, intrinsic-va
 lue (why democracy is the best choice)\, collectivist (the importance of d
 iverse voices)\, individual (how democracy is empowering) and process-orie
 nted (showing democratic institutions work)\; a short creative brief gives
  further guidance. AWARDS: 4 winning entries receive USD $1\,000 each\; 5 
 honorable mentions receive USD $500 each\; all winners receive amplificati
 on across People Powered and Democracy Narratives Alliance communications 
 networks and are featured in a global screening event. WHO CAN APPLY: indi
 vidual creators and small teams anywhere in the world\; especially welcome
 s creators who reach audiences outside usual democracy circles\, who come 
 from communities traditionally with less of a say in public discourse\, an
 d who work in civic tech\, advocacy\, community organising\, comedy\, art\
 , illustration\, games\, or lifestyle content. Applications opened 14 July
  2026 and are submitted through the official form on the People Powered De
 mocracy Narratives Alliance page. Submission deadline: 14 September 2026. 
 Winners announced October-November 2026. Note: the initiative was itself v
 oted into existence through People Powered's participatory budgeting proce
 ss (PP Decides).\n\nOrganisation: People Powered and Democracy Narratives 
 Alliance\nLocation: Worldwide\; remote. Global screening event for winners
 .\nAward: USD $1\,000 for each of 4 winners\; USD $500 for each of 5 honor
 able mentions. Plus amplification across People Powered and Democracy Narr
 atives Alliance networks and feature in a global screening event. No appli
 cation fee.\nDuration: One-time award.\n\nMore: https://www.peoplepowered.
 org/news-content/announcing-the-democracy-stories-lab?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.peoplepowered.org/news-content/announcing-the-democracy-sto
 ries-lab?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; remote. Global screening event for winners.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-inc-year-13-mentor-open-call-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260708
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260709
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEW INC Year 13 Mentor Open Call
DESCRIPTION:NEW INC\, the New Museum's cultural incubator\, is seeking ment
 ors for Year 13 of its program supporting creative practitioners at the in
 tersection of art\, design\, and technology. This is a call for mentors (c
 reative and business leaders)\, not for program members. TWO MENTOR TYPES:
  Dedicated Mentors ($500 honorarium) are matched one-on-one with a member 
 and commit to ongoing support throughout the year\; Office Hours Mentors (
 $300 honorarium) offer one-on-one sessions to the broader community on an 
 as-needed basis. All mentors receive a one-year All Access Plus membership
  to the New Museum\, invitations to special events including the annual DE
 MO Festival\, and access to community-only workshops\, talks\, and social 
 events. YEAR 13 PROGRAM TRACKS mentors may support: Art & Code\; Creative 
 Science\; Extended Realities\; Launchpad\; Social Architecture. KEY DATES:
  Mentor Open Call closes 8 July 2026\; Mentor Open House (virtual info ses
 sion) 17 June 2026\, 12-1pm ET (register via the site)\; mandatory Dedicat
 ed Mentor matchmaking ranking session 11 September 2026\; mandatory Dedica
 ted Mentor matchmaking event 18 September 2026\; Dedicated Mentor matches 
 announced 2 October 2026. Most NEW INC activities take place Monday-Friday
 \, 10am-6pm ET. Apply at https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/.\n\n
 Organisation: NEW INC (New Museum Incubator)\nLocation: New York\, NY\, US
 A\; primarily in-person at 250 Hudson St\, lower Manhattan. Some hybrid pa
 rticipation possible.\nAward: Dedicated Mentors: $500 honorarium. Office H
 ours Mentors: $300 honorarium. All mentors also receive a one-year All Acc
 ess Plus membership to the New Museum. No application fee.\nDuration: Year
  13 program (September 2026 onward)\; average 4-8 hours of engagement per 
 week.\n\nMore: https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/?utm_source=art
 ificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.newmuseum.org/new-inc/open-call/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:New York\, NY\, USA\; primarily in-person at 250 Hudson St\, lower
  Manhattan. Some hybrid participation possible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cht-emerging-voices-ai-society-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github
 .io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Center for Humane Technology: Emerging Voices in AI and S
 ociety Fellowship (Inaugural Cohort)
DESCRIPTION:CHT's inaugural Emerging Voices in AI and Society Fellowship pl
 aces 3 fellows (one per focus area) in a 6-month\, part-time program to de
 velop rigorous public-impact research and writing on how AI is reshaping s
 ociety. Fellows choose from 5 focus areas: (1) AI and Cognition — what A
 I does to human judgment\, critical thinking\, and intellectual independen
 ce\; (2) AI and Relationships — AI's effects on intimacy\, companionship
 \, and social bonds\; (3) AI and Surveillance — how AI enables monitorin
 g\, tracking\, and control\; (4) AI and Spirituality — AI's intersection
  with meaning\, transcendence\, and religious practice\; (5) Inside the Ma
 chine: AI From a Technologist's Perspective. Up to 2 focus areas per appli
 cation. DELIVERABLES: one flagship research piece\, report\, or long-form 
 essay for public impact\; 1-3 op-eds (placed in target outlets or CHT Subs
 tack)\; one podcast appearance (Your Undivided Attention or equivalent)\; 
 cohort meetings and bi-weekly check-ins. SUPPORT: bi-weekly guidance from 
 Senior Director of Strategy and Impact\; editorial\, communications and gr
 aphic design support\; media training and PR placement support\; CHT email
  address and Slack access\; listing on CHT's public fellows page. WHO SHOU
 LD APPLY: mid-career researchers (psychology\, sociology\, philosophy\, co
 gnitive science\, communications\, STS)\, policy analysts\, journalists co
 vering tech and society\, civil society advocates\, or technologists with 
 insider perspective. Strong preference for those with a public writing tra
 ck record (op-eds\, Substack\, peer-reviewed work) and some public presenc
 e. HOW TO APPLY: 1-2 page research proposal per topic (up to 2)\, CV/resum
 e (2 pages max)\, 2-minute video explaining fit\, writing sample. Deadline
 : Sunday 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EST.\n\nOrganisation: Center for Humane Te
 chnology (CHT)\nLocation: Remote\, 10 hours/week overlapping with EST requ
 ired.\nAward: $30\,000 fixed stipend paid in three milestone-based tranche
 s. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months: September 14\, 2026 through Ma
 rch 18\, 2027. 15 hours per week.\n\nMore: https://www.humanetech.com/fell
 owship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.humanetech.com/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, 10 hours/week overlapping with EST required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:horizon-europe-cl2-2026-heritage-ai-creative@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260923
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260924
SUMMARY:Deadline: Horizon Europe Cluster 2: Culture\, Creativity and Inclus
 ive Society (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE) — incl. AI and Creative Conte
 nt topic
DESCRIPTION:Horizon Europe Cluster 2 funds research on culture\, creativity
 \, and inclusive society. The 2026 call (HORIZON-CL2-2026-01-HERITAGE\, la
 unched May 12\, 2026) covers 7 topics with an indicative budget of €85.5
  million. Topic HERITAGE-04 is explicitly titled 'Towards a fair and trans
 parent market for cultural and creative content in the era of generative A
 I\,' covering: impacts of generative AI on creative industries employment\
 ; upskilling needs for creative workers\; policy and regulatory frameworks
 \; fair remuneration and transparency. Highly relevant for research into A
 I and cultural/creative industries\, artists' rights in the AI era\, and s
 ociotechnical studies of AI and culture. ELIGIBILITY: consortium-based app
 lications (minimum 3 organisations from 3 different EU/associated countrie
 s are typical for Horizon RIA grants\; check call conditions on the Fundin
 g & Tenders portal for specific requirements). Deadline: 23 September 2026
 . Apply via the EU Funding & Tenders portal (ec.europa.eu/info/funding-ten
 ders).\n\nOrganisation: European Research Executive Agency (REA) / Europea
 n Commission\, Horizon Europe\nLocation: European Union and associated cou
 ntries. Consortium-based\; typically requires partners from multiple EU/Ho
 rizon member states.\nAward: Indicative total budget €85.5 million acros
 s 7 topics (2026 call). Individual project amounts vary by topic. Up to 10
 0% funding rate for non-profit research organizations. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Typically 3-4 years\; varies by topic.\n\nMore: https://rea.ec
 .europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-culture-creativity-
 and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cultural-and-creative
 -industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://rea.ec.europa.eu/funding-and-grants/horizon-europe-cluster-2-cu
 lture-creativity-and-inclusive-society/european-cultural-heritage-and-cult
 ural-and-creative-industries_en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
LOCATION:European Union and associated countries. Consortium-based\; typica
 lly requires partners from multiple EU/Horizon member states.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:omidyar-network-tech-journalism-fund-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260904T211013Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261231
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270101
SUMMARY:Deadline: Omidyar Network: Tech Journalism Fund
DESCRIPTION:The Omidyar Network Tech Journalism Fund offers rolling grants 
 of $5\,000-$25\,000 for technology journalism projects. Relevant for journ
 alists and media organizations investigating AI\, tech policy\, and AI's i
 mpacts on culture\, society\, and creative industries. Applications are ac
 cepted on a rolling basis through December 31\, 2026 — one of the few cu
 rrently open opportunities with a confirmed near-term deadline. Apply via 
 the application form on omidyar.com.\n\nOrganisation: Omidyar Network\nLoc
 ation: Open to journalists and media organizations worldwide.\nAward: $5\,
 000-$25\,000 per project. Rolling deadline through December 31\, 2026. No 
 application fee.\nDuration: Per-project\; scope and timeline defined in th
 e proposal.\n\nMore: https://omidyar.com/tech-journalism-fund-application/
 ?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://omidyar.com/tech-journalism-fund-application/?utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to journalists and media organizations worldwide.
